            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we found some substantial air leakage in the following locations:



** The old chimney location above the fancoil appears to be open into the attic, and we also found substantial air coming in at the floor level vent in the hallway outside your master bedroom.



** The chimney chase for the wood stove is also not sealed where it enters the attic, and substantial air flow was noticed in the chase enclosure inside the closet where the attic access is located.



** Pictures that I took inside the attic illustrate the high level of air flow (and dirt deposit) going into the attic from the 2 chimney issues listed above. The roof vents along the peak of the roof show the snow melt on the roof from the heated air leakage going into the attic space.



** The combustion air supply &amp; chimney pipes for the current boiler are supposed to be connected in a sealed &amp; direct vent configuration, however the dedicated combustion air pipe has been removed from the oil burner. This open combustion air pipe represents air leakage from your house on windy winter days, whenever the boiler is not in operation.



** The rear door from the heated section of your house has alot of air leakage around the perimeter of the door.



** The current wood stove appears to have a combustion air opening into the bottom of the unit (behind the ash bin drawer) that is not directly sealed to the wood stove, and the hearth around the base of the woodstove was quite cold &amp; we could feel the air leakage.



Savings: With your house being quite drafty (&gt; 10 air changes per hour on a windy winter day = 1 full air change every 6 minutes), I think you can achieve substantial air leakage reduction &amp; energy/cost savings if you were to reduce this by 50% (even at 50%, this house would still have 300% more air leakage than most new construction houses in Yellowknife). Achieving a 50% reduction in air leakage is estimated to potentially save $ 1,588 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 46.2 GJ.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings />
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation />
                <windows>

The windows in your home are original to 1983, and they were really good quality triple pane wood windows. Most of them are fixed pane (picture) windows, so there is very little air leakage through the frame. They do not have any low-E coating or insulating gas fill, however still appear to be in good shape.



For your longer term energy efficiency planning, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of these older windows to ENERGY STARÂ® qualified units rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows.



Savings:

By replacing all of the older windows with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified units rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially save $ 166 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 4.6 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows.

</windows>
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house currently has a 184 litre electric hot water tank. Should you consider a switch to a 95% AFUE condensing &amp; modulating propane boiler, we talked about an opportunity for you to have the boiler also heat your domestic hot water year round. The savings for this are outlined within the heating system upgrade section of my report.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil boiler was designed to be a direct vent unit, however the dedicated combustion air supply pipe to the burner has been disconnected. Assuming the boiler was still set up in direct vent, sealed combustion mode the manufacturer brochure indicates an 87% Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency (AFUE). This oil boiler was also once set up to also heat your domestic hot water during the heating season, however the connections for this "combi loop" have been turned off - so the hot water tank operates in "all electric" mode all year.



The oil boiler heats some sections of the house with radiator loops around the perimeter walls, and also incorporates an air exchanger (connected to your thermostat) for heated air circulation to other sections of the house. In conjunction with this heating system upgrade, your contractor can look at the location of thermostats that operate the various loops to ensure they best serve the locations of the house they are intended to heat.



I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing &amp; modulating propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. This new boiler can also supply the domestic hot water and can be used year round as the new boiler would be modulating to adjust to the combined heating &amp; hot water needs. The efficiency of the domestic hot water portion would be approximately a 0.79 Energy Factor (EF).



Savings:

By replacing your current oil boiler with a high-efficiency condensing propane unit with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially save $ 924 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 19.4 GJ, and you would qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). In addition, there is another estimated $ 963 of savings per year for the domestic hot water.



In addition, I modeled an upgrade to a new EEIP qualified wood stove that has a dedicated combustion air sealed directly to the firebox. You could potentially save another $60 per year, decrease your annual energy usage by another 1.9 GJ, and qualify for 1/3 of the purchase cost up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP)



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely with the short and dark days of winter. Although winter may provide little or no usable energy (depending on your latitude in the NWT), the long hours of sunlight in spring, summer and fall can provide significant solar heating and electricity generation opportunities for up to eight months of the year.  As Solar Photovoltaic (PV) system prices continue to drop, they become more competitive with the cost of grid-supplied and especially off-grid generation.



Savings:

By installing a 3 kW solar photovoltaic system on the front "south facing" roof of your house, flush mounted on the roof, you could potentially save about $ 1,123 per year in electricity costs.

</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has two toilets and one of them is an older 13 litre model, and you should consider replacing it with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush !!



Savings:

By replacing a toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

Depressurization Test (not performed)



Your house currently has no equipment exhausting to the outside, as the range hood vents inside, the one bathroom fan on the main floor does not seem to work (perhaps disconnected) and the dryer is venting inside. As you make your house more air tight, the relative humidity in the house will climb a bit and you will likely want to vent the dryer to the outside. You may also want to have a bathroom fan vented to the outside in the bathroom where showers are usually taken. The range hood fan likely does not need to be vented out as long as the charcoal filter is maintained and you are not cooking an excessive amount of meals that produce steam.



During my assessment of your home, there appeared to be functioning smoke or carbon monoxide (CO) detectors (I think it was a CO upstairs connected to the NEST). Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Yellowknife



The following Nov. 1, 2018 values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $18.70 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.51 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



